Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. *This is not legal advice. I do
not offer legal advice. If you need legal advice seek legal counsel.
Please be advised, the information shared in this guide is for
educational and  informational purposes only.* 

Dispute 21 - Day 6:

Defeating Debt Collectors Pt. II Continued

Bruce, in yesterday's lesson we focused on 2 ways to defeat Debt
Collectors, with one of the strongest weapons being the debt validation
letter. I even gave you a debt validation letter crafted on the
foundation of the law that you can use and customize to your liking to
tackle stubborn collections and annoying and abusive collection
agencies. So if you haven't checked out that lesson yet, be sure to
review that content as debt validation is key in the repair and dispute
process.

I coach a lot of credit repair specialists and consult for many other
companies alike, and one of the concerns or questions that frequently
come up during the debt validation discussion is handling credit card
debt and why negative credit card accounts are often times more
difficult to deal with. Even in general industry conversation people
often say, Elle, 

**I've heard Credit Card Debt Validation is Different from Other Types
of Validation. Is This True?**So, in today's lesson we will address this
topic.

Bruce, as you know, legally, you have the right to dispute and
deny a debt until someone can prove in a court of law with all the
relevant documents that it is your debt. 

Frequently, this proof requires a signed contract. 

However, for credit cards, there is no signed contract. Nameless,
unsigned, frequently wrongly-dated or undated contracts are the standard
consumer credit card agreement for credit card banks. So when it comes
to collecting defaulted credit card debt, they need to get you to admit
to the debt, so they can "re-contract" with you and not have you denying
and disputing the debt.

Before the Consumer Finance Protection Bureau was taken over by the
Trump administration it was attempting to include original creditors
like credit card banks as debt collectors subject to the FDCPA. Some
states like Massachusetts and California do consider credit card banks
debt collectors. But regardless of their FDCPA status as debt
collectors, you should send the credit card bank the letter we broke
down in day 5 or something similar to it along with the insertion below
denying and disputing the debt and asking for a full GAAP accounting.
This creates a written record of dispute that could make it harder for a
collection attorney to successfully sue you in court for the debt.

To validate, the debt collector or collection attorney must go to the
original creditor, the credit card bank; who because of its computerized
record keeping has trouble accounting for all the entries that make up
the current amount of a credit card debt; who, by practice, has no
signed contract with the consumer's name on it; and who does not want
to be bothered fulfilling individual document requests from a debt
collector.

Validation Letter Language Insertion when Disputing Credit Card Bank
Debt

[The sample letter language provided below in red is based on
Massachusetts State Law. I advise that you search your / your client's
state debt collection laws for proper verbiage. They may have something
like this.] 

I also expect you to provide a complete ledger and accounting of debits
and credits per GAAP principles for my inspection as required by
Commonwealth of Massachusetts regulations 940 CMR 7.08-- 

940 CMR 7.00: Debt Collection Regulations; 7.08: Inspection 

* It shall constitute an unfair or deceptive act or practice for a
creditor to fail to allow a debtor or an attorney for a debtor to
inspect and copy the following materials regarding a debt during normal
business hours of the creditor and upon notice given to such creditor
not less than five business days preceding the scheduled inspection: 

1) All papers or copies of papers in the possession of the creditor
which bear the signature of the debtor and which concern the debt being
collected; 

2) A ledger, account card, or similar record in the possession of a
creditor which reflects the date and amount of payments, credits, and
charges concerning the debt. 

Consider this document notice that I am exercising my right to inspect
all the papers and records itemized in (1) and (2) above. 

Please write me at your earliest convenience to confirm my review of
these alleged records. I want to inspect these alleged records the last
week of September 200X within my respective court district in eastern
Massachusetts. Consider this notice given not less than five days
preceding my scheduled inspection of said records. 

[As advised above, you will need to check your state's debt collection
laws to find out if there is a gem like this in them. If not, discard
this language. Everything else below and in the Debt Validation Letter
provided in Day 5 is strong enough to leave a lasting, comparative
impression with the debt collector.]

BONUS:
More Powerful Language to include in Your Debt Validation Letter (to be
inserted at the end of the day 5 letter) Everything in blue is what you
would include. Everything in black is an explanation of "why" and
"what."

Please also be aware that if any negative mark is found on my credit
reports from your company or any company that you represent, this will
result in my immediate filing of a lawsuit against you and your client
for: 

1) Violation of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, 

2) Violation of the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act, 

3) Defamation of Character, 

4) Negligent Enablement of Identity Fraud.

[You are noticing them that you understand under the FDCPA the
collection activities you have asked them to stop include putting a
negative listing for the debt in question on your credit reports.] 

You are instructed to take no action that could be detrimental to any of
my credit reports. 

In addition, [insert your state's Office of Business Regulation] has
no record of your firm being licensed in [insert your/your client's
state] as a debt collector. As you know, there are penalties for
fraudulent collection activities. I am in possession of documents from
you addressed to me in [insert your/your client's state] clearly marked
"this is an attempt to collect a debt," which are evidence of your
fraudulent activities. 

[Based on experience, 50 percent of the debt collectors who contact our
clients are out of state and not licensed to collect debts in the
client's state (Keep in mind some are licensed in the state). My
state's Secretary of State's government web site has a list of debt
collectors licensed by the state. You should check your state's
licensing policy for debt collectors and for where to find the list of
licensed debt collectors. Omit the portion above if they are in fact
licensed to collect debts in you or your client's state.] 

You are guilty of unfair and deceptive practices under [your/your
client's state] law. This is notice to you to cease and desist from
those practices. You are injuring me by causing me to respond to these
bogus collection attempts. 

[Every state has a consumer protection law that prohibits 

**deceptive practices**, and many prohibit 

**unfair** or unconscionable 

**practices** as well. These statutes, commonly known as 

**Unfair and Deceptive Acts and Practices** or UDAP statutes, provide
bedrock protections for consumers. Check your state's UDAP
statutes.]  

[To pursue a debt collector in civil court for UDAP violations, you must
first write them to cease and desist, using certified return receipt.
Then if they persist, you can sue them.] 

I suggest you get your records in order before I have to proceed with
legal action. 

Signed, 
your name and address 

[Many warn about actually signing a written communication to a debt
collector for fear they might forge your name on a document that
furthers their collection goal. You can simply type or print the name or
if you choose to provide a signature, you or your client can use a pen
stroke that does not in any way resemble the true signature.]

Bruce,  

In Day 7, we will complete the Debt Validation lessons and move on to
the FCRA, dealing directly with creditors and furnishers, creating a
dispute flow + many other bonus emails of content to build your
understanding of the credit repair and dispute process. 

